PG.notes("s15-random", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains Math.random() and the scale-floor-shift recipe for producing whole numbers in a range, notes that the numbers are pseudo-random, then has the learner build a love calculator that shows a random 1-100 score.</p>\n<h3>Math.random()</h3>\n<ul><li>Math.random() is a built-in function taking no inputs; the parentheses are still required to call it.</li><li>It returns a floating-point number from 0 up to but not including 1 (up to about 16 decimal places such as 0.9999...). 0 can occur; 1 never does.</li><li>A new value is produced on every call, which makes it useful for games, dice and similar features.</li><li>The MDN page for Math.random describes it as pseudo-random with the range [0, 1) and says to scale it to the range you need.</li></ul>\n<h3>Scale, floor, shift recipe</h3>\n<ul><li>Multiply by the number of outcomes wanted: n * 6 gives 0 to 5.999...</li><li>Wrap in Math.floor() to drop the decimals, giving whole numbers 0 to 5. The result can never be 6.</li><li>Add 1 to move the range to 1 through 6. In general: Math.floor(Math.random() * count) + lowest.</li><li>Whole numbers are almost always what you want, so floor is nearly always applied.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pseudo-random numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Computers are deterministic, so they produce numbers with algorithms that merely look statistically random; hence pseudo-random.</li><li>The lecture points to an extra-reading video in the resources and treats it as optional.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Dice roll, 1 to 6</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var n = Math.random();      // 0 &lt;= n &lt; 1\nn = n * 6;                  // 0 &lt;= n &lt; 6\nn = Math.floor(n);          // 0..5\nn = n + 1;                  // 1..6\nconsole.log(n);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Love Calculator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> An empty script file (or browser DevTools snippet).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Ask for two names, ignore them, and alert a random love score between 1 and 100.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Call prompt() twice, once for each person's name. The answers are not stored because they are never used.</li><li>Create a variable loveScore from Math.random().</li><li>Multiply it by 100, then wrap in Math.floor() to get whole numbers 0 to 99.</li><li>Add 1 so the range becomes 1 to 100.</li><li>Show the result with alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveScore + &quot;%&quot;).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Two prompt boxes appear, then an alert with a different whole-number percentage between 1 and 100 on each run.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(&quot;Welcome to the love calculator&quot;);\nprompt(&quot;Enter your name&quot;);\nprompt(&quot;Enter your crush's name&quot;);\nvar loveCalculator = Math.random();\nloveCalculator = loveCalculator * 100;\nloveCalculator = Math.floor(loveCalculator) + 1;\nalert(&quot;The chance of you staying together is &quot; + loveCalculator + &quot;%&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the parentheses on Math.random, which gives the function rather than a number.</li><li>Forgetting + 1, so the range is 0-99 and can never reach 100.</li><li>Using Math.round instead of floor, which skews the ends of the range.</li><li>Expecting the maximum value to equal the multiplier.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-if-else", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces control flow with if/else: a condition in parentheses decides which block runs. Applied to the love calculator so a high score gets a special message.</p>\n<h3>Control flow and conditionals</h3>\n<ul><li>Control flow means the program takes different paths depending on current conditions.</li><li>if evaluates a condition. When it is true the block that follows runs; else holds the block for when it is false.</li><li>Syntax: if (condition) { ... } else { ... }. The condition sits in parentheses, each branch in curly braces.</li><li>Convention is to put the opening brace on the same line as if and else, which keeps long blocks readable.</li><li>Only one of the two blocks runs for any given evaluation.</li></ul>\n<h3>Conditions in the love calculator</h3>\n<ul><li>Equality is tested with three equals signs: loveScore === 100. This only triggers for one value in a hundred, so it is awkward to test.</li><li>Testing a range is more practical, using a comparator such as greater than: loveScore &gt; 70.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A single = assigns a value and must not be used as a comparison inside if.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Generic shape</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">if (track === &quot;clear&quot;) {\n  goStraight();\n} else {\n  turnRight();\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Love calculator with a conditional</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var loveScore = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1;\nif (loveScore &gt; 70) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveScore + &quot;%, indicating genuine love&quot;);\n} else {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveScore + &quot;%&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add a special message to the love calculator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The love calculator from the previous lecture.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show a different alert when the score is high.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Keep the prompts and the 1-100 loveScore calculation.</li><li>Wrap the alert in if (loveScore &gt; 70) with an extended message.</li><li>Put the plain message in the else block.</li><li>Run several times to see both branches.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Scores above 70 show the special message; others show the plain one.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var loveCalculator = Math.random() * 100;\nloveCalculator = Math.floor(loveCalculator) + 1;\nif (loveCalculator &gt; 70) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveCalculator + &quot;%, indicating genuine love&quot;);\n} else {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveCalculator + &quot;%&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using = instead of === in a condition.</li><li>Testing for a single exact value when a range is wanted.</li><li>Forgetting braces or parentheses around the condition.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-comparators", "<p class=\"summary\">Lists the comparison operators usable in conditions and explains the difference between strict (===) and loose (==) equality using a number versus a numeric string.</p>\n<h3>Comparison operators</h3>\n<ul><li>=== is true when both sides are equal; !== is true when they differ.</li><li>&gt; and &lt; test greater than and less than; &gt;= and &lt;= also accept equality.</li><li>Each yields a boolean, which if/else statements use to decide the branch.</li></ul>\n<h3>=== versus ==</h3>\n<ul><li>With a = 1 and b = &quot;1&quot;, typeof a is &quot;number&quot; and typeof b is &quot;string&quot;.</li><li>a === b is false because strict equality compares the data types as well as the values.</li><li>a == b is true because loose equality ignores type and converts first.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Prefer === and !== by default to avoid type-coercion surprises (0 == &quot;&quot; is true, for example).</li></ul>\n<h3>Looking ahead</h3>\n<ul><li>To give the love calculator separate messages for low, middle and high scores, comparators must be combined, which the next lecture covers.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Strict vs loose equality</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var a = 1;\nvar b = &quot;1&quot;;\nconsole.log(typeof a, typeof b);   // number string\nif (a === b) { console.log(&quot;yes&quot;); } else { console.log(&quot;no&quot;); }   // no\nif (a == b)  { console.log(&quot;yes&quot;); } else { console.log(&quot;no&quot;); }   // yes</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference-repo checks</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log(5 === &quot;5&quot;);  // false\nconsole.log(5 !== &quot;5&quot;);  // true\nconsole.log(10 &gt;= 10);   // true\nconsole.log(10 &lt; 5);     // false</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using == and silently matching values of different types.</li><li>Confusing = (assign) with == / ===.</li><li>Mixing up &gt; with &gt;= at range boundaries.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-bmi-advanced", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows the three logical operators (&amp;&amp;, ||, !) and uses &amp;&amp; to give the love calculator three message bands: low, middle and high.</p>\n<h3>Logical operators</h3>\n<ul><li>&amp;&amp; (AND) is true only when both conditions are true.</li><li>|| (OR), typed with two pipe characters, is true when at least one condition is true.</li><li>! (NOT) inverts a boolean.</li><li>They combine several comparisons into one condition inside if (...).</li></ul>\n<h3>Three bands for the love score</h3>\n<ul><li>Above 70: the special message.</li><li>Greater than 30 AND at most 70: the plain score alert.</li><li>At most 30: a message that the pair mixes badly.</li><li>The reference solution uses three separate if statements. Using &gt;= and &lt;= carefully keeps boundary values 30 and 70 in exactly one band.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> An else if chain would also work and avoids rechecking conditions.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Three bands</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">if (loveScore &gt; 70) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveScore + &quot;%, indicating genuine love&quot;);\n}\nif (loveScore &gt; 30 &amp;&amp; loveScore &lt;= 70) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveScore + &quot;%&quot;);\n}\nif (loveScore &lt;= 30) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveScore + &quot;%, you go together like oil and water&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Extend the love calculator to three bands</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Love calculator with a single if/else on loveScore &gt; 70.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Give different alerts for scores above 70, from 31 to 70 and 30 or below.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Keep the score generation and prompts.</li><li>Add a condition combining loveScore &gt; 30 and loveScore &lt;= 70 with &amp;&amp;.</li><li>Add one for loveScore &lt;= 30.</li><li>Run repeatedly to see all three messages.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Exactly one of three alerts appears depending on the score.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var loveCalculator = Math.random() * 100;\nloveCalculator = Math.floor(loveCalculator) + 1;\nif (loveCalculator &gt; 70) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveCalculator + &quot;%, indicating genuine love&quot;);\n}\nif (loveCalculator &gt; 30 &amp;&amp; loveCalculator &lt;= 70) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveCalculator + &quot;%&quot;);\n}\nif (loveCalculator &lt;= 30) {\n  alert(&quot;Your love score is &quot; + loveCalculator + &quot;%, you go together like oil and water&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Overlapping or gapped ranges, e.g. &gt; 30 and &lt; 70 loses exactly 70.</li><li>Writing a single | or &amp; instead of || and &amp;&amp;.</li><li>Typing 30 &lt; loveScore &lt; 70, which does not work as a range check.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-leap-year", "<p class=\"summary\">Sets out the leap year rules, shows a flowchart of the decision order, and explains the exact output strings the next exercise expects.</p>\n<h3>Leap year rules</h3>\n<ul><li>A year divisible by 4 is a leap year.</li><li>Exception: a year also divisible by 100 is not a leap year.</li><li>Exception to the exception: a year also divisible by 400 is a leap year.</li><li>Example: 2000 divides by 4, by 100 and by 400, so it is a leap year. 1900 divides by 4 and 100 but not 400, so it is not.</li><li>Background: leap years compensate for the mismatch between the calendar and the Earth's orbit; the exercise links an explainer video and a reference site that steps through the rules.</li></ul>\n<h3>Flowchart approach</h3>\n<ul><li>Draw the logic as a flowchart (draw.io) before coding.</li><li>Step 1: divisible by 4? If no, not a leap year.</li><li>Step 2: divisible by 100? If no, leap year.</li><li>Step 3: divisible by 400? If yes, leap year; if no, not a leap year.</li><li>Each decision diamond maps to an if statement, so the chart can be translated straight into code.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Leap Year challenge</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Exercise page with a function to write that receives a year.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Return the exact string &quot;Leap year.&quot; or &quot;Not leap year.&quot; for the given year.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Study the rules and examples.</li><li>Optionally draw your own flowchart.</li><li>Write the logic using the modulo operator (year % 4 === 0 and so on) inside if/else.</li><li>Match the output text exactly, including capital letter, space and full stop, or the tests fail.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> 2000 gives &quot;Leap year.&quot;; 1900 and 2023 give &quot;Not leap year.&quot;</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Checking 100 before 4, or forgetting the 400 exception.</li><li>Typos in the returned strings.</li><li>Using = instead of === in the comparisons.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l104", "<p class=\"summary\">Translates the leap year flowchart into nested if statements using the modulo operator, one decision per nesting level.</p>\n<h3>From flowchart to nested ifs</h3>\n<ul><li>Each question in the flowchart becomes an if; each answer path becomes a branch.</li><li>Modulo (%) returns the remainder of a division, so year % 4 === 0 means 'divides cleanly by 4'.</li><li>Level 1: if year % 4 === 0 continue; else return &quot;Not leap year.&quot;.</li><li>Level 2 (inside level 1): if year % 100 === 0 continue; else return &quot;Leap year.&quot;.</li><li>Level 3 (inside level 2): if year % 400 === 0 return &quot;Leap year.&quot;; else return &quot;Not leap year.&quot;.</li><li>Every path ends in a return with the exact required string.</li><li>Going from the written rules to code is a big jump; the flowchart is the stepping stone, and the logic is the hard part.</li></ul>\n<h3>Compact alternative</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo collapses the logic into one condition: (year % 4 === 0 &amp;&amp; year % 100 !== 0) || year % 400 === 0. It is equivalent but denser to read.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Nested version mirroring the flowchart</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function isLeap(year) {\n  if (year % 4 === 0) {\n    if (year % 100 === 0) {\n      if (year % 400 === 0) {\n        return &quot;Leap year.&quot;;\n      } else {\n        return &quot;Not leap year.&quot;;\n      }\n    } else {\n      return &quot;Leap year.&quot;;\n    }\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Not leap year.&quot;;\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference-repo single-condition version</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function leapYear(year) {\n  if ((year % 4 === 0 &amp;&amp; year % 100 !== 0) || year % 400 === 0) {\n    return &quot;Leap year.&quot;;\n  } else {\n    return &quot;Not leap year.&quot;;\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Leap Year (solution)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Function receiving year with the flowchart as a guide.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Return &quot;Leap year.&quot; or &quot;Not leap year.&quot;.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Check year % 4 === 0; otherwise return Not leap year.</li><li>Inside, check year % 100 === 0; otherwise return Leap year.</li><li>Inside that, check year % 400 === 0 to choose between the two results.</li><li>Run the course's check and confirm all tests pass.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> 2000 and 2024 give Leap year.; 1900 and 2023 give Not leap year.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function isLeap(year) {\n  if (year % 4 === 0) {\n    if (year % 100 === 0) {\n      return year % 400 === 0 ? &quot;Leap year.&quot; : &quot;Not leap year.&quot;;\n    }\n    return &quot;Leap year.&quot;;\n  }\n  return &quot;Not leap year.&quot;;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Missing the else path so the function returns undefined for some years.</li><li>Wrong nesting order of 4, 100 and 400.</li><li>Differences in the output strings.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-arrays", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces arrays as a way to keep many related values in one variable, covering literal syntax, zero-based indexing, length and includes(). Exercise: a birthday-party guest list checker.</p>\n<h3>Why arrays</h3>\n<ul><li>A variable so far held one value. Checking a name against many names with a long chain of if/else if would be unmanageable.</li><li>An array is an ordered collection of related items stored in one variable (an egg box compared with a single egg).</li></ul>\n<h3>Syntax and indexing</h3>\n<ul><li>Create with square brackets and comma-separated items: var eggs = [item0, item1, ...].</li><li>Read an item with name[position]. Square brackets both create and access arrays, a hint that you are dealing with a collection.</li><li>Positions start at 0, so eggs[1] is the second item and guestList[0] the first.</li><li>array.length returns the number of items. Logging the whole array in the console also shows its size.</li><li>Strings in the array need quotes.</li></ul>\n<h3>includes()</h3>\n<ul><li>array.includes(value) searches the array and returns true if any element matches, otherwise false.</li><li>The boolean result is directly usable as an if condition.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Matching is strict and case-sensitive: &quot;angela&quot; does not match &quot;Angela&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Array basics</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var guestList = [&quot;Wesley&quot;, &quot;Maria&quot;, &quot;Michele&quot;, &quot;Eveline&quot;, &quot;Levi&quot;, &quot;Everaldo&quot;];\nconsole.log(guestList);         // whole array\nconsole.log(guestList.length);  // 6\nconsole.log(guestList[0]);      // &quot;Wesley&quot;\nconsole.log(guestList.includes(&quot;Maria&quot;));   // true</pre>\n<h2>The task: Birthday party guest list</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script with an array of guest names.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Ask for the visitor's name and check the guest list.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create the guestList array of strings.</li><li>Store the answer of prompt(&quot;What is your name?&quot;) in a variable such as guestName.</li><li>Use guestList.includes(guestName) as the if condition.</li><li>Alert a welcome message when true; alert a polite refusal in the else branch.</li><li>Test with a listed name and a random one.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Names on the list get a welcome alert; any other input gets the refusal alert.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(&quot;Welcome to my birthday party&quot;);\nvar name = prompt(&quot;Enter your first name so I can see if you are on the guest list&quot;);\n\nvar guestList = [&quot;Wesley&quot;, &quot;Maria&quot;, &quot;Michele&quot;, &quot;Eveline&quot;, &quot;Levi&quot;, &quot;Everaldo&quot;];\n\nif (guestList.includes(name)) {\n  alert(&quot;You are on the list, welcome&quot;);\n} else {\n  alert(&quot;You are not on the list&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Counting from 1 instead of 0.</li><li>Forgetting commas or quotes in the array literal.</li><li>Case or whitespace differences making includes() return false.</li><li>Reference repo includes an author footer in index.html that should be ignored.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-lunch", "<p class=\"summary\">Uses the FizzBuzz interview problem to teach push() and pop(), a counter variable, the modulo operator for divisibility, and why the order of if / else if branches matters. Built incrementally as a function that adds one more item to an array on each call.</p>\n<h3>The FizzBuzz problem</h3>\n<ul><li>Produce the numbers 1 to 100, but a multiple of 3 becomes &quot;Fizz&quot;, a multiple of 5 becomes &quot;Buzz&quot;, and a multiple of both (15, 30...) becomes &quot;FizzBuzz&quot;.</li><li>It is a common screening question because it quickly shows whether a candidate can break a problem into steps.</li><li>In this lecture it is built one step at a time: one new item per call of fizzBuzz().</li></ul>\n<h3>push() and pop()</h3>\n<ul><li>Arrays can start empty: var output = [];</li><li>array.push(value) appends value to the END of the array, never the start or middle. It can be called repeatedly to grow the array.</li><li>array.pop() removes the last item from the array (and returns it).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> shift() and unshift() do the same jobs at the front of the array.</li></ul>\n<h3>Counter variable</h3>\n<ul><li>Calling output.push(1) repeatedly would keep adding 1. Instead keep a separate variable count (starting at 1), push count, then increase it with count++ (same as count = count + 1).</li><li>Declare output and count outside the function so they persist between calls; if declared inside they would be reset on every call.</li></ul>\n<h3>Modulo for divisibility</h3>\n<ul><li>a % b gives the remainder of a divided by b. 12 % 2 is 0 (divides cleanly); 12 % 5 is 2.</li><li>count % 3 === 0 therefore means count is a multiple of 3; likewise for 5.</li><li>Replace the pushed value with the matching word when the test is true.</li></ul>\n<h3>Order of conditions</h3>\n<ul><li>An if / else if / else chain runs the first branch whose condition is true and skips the rest.</li><li>If the Fizz test comes first, 15 matches it and Buzz is never examined, so 15 shows as Fizz.</li><li>Fix: test the combined case first: if (count % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; count % 5 === 0) push &quot;FizzBuzz&quot;; else if divisible by 3 push &quot;Fizz&quot;; else if divisible by 5 push &quot;Buzz&quot;; else push count.</li><li>General rule: put the most specific condition first.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">push and pop</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var output = [];\noutput.push(1);\noutput.push(2);\nconsole.log(output);   // [1, 2]\noutput.pop();\nconsole.log(output);   // [1]</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final one-step-per-call FizzBuzz</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var output = [];\nvar count = 1;\n\nfunction fizzBuzz() {\n  if (count % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; count % 5 === 0) {\n    output.push(&quot;FizzBuzz&quot;);\n  } else if (count % 3 === 0) {\n    output.push(&quot;Fizz&quot;);\n  } else if (count % 5 === 0) {\n    output.push(&quot;Buzz&quot;);\n  } else {\n    output.push(count);\n  }\n  count++;\n  console.log(output);\n}\n\nfizzBuzz();   // [1]\nfizzBuzz();   // [1, 2]\nfizzBuzz();   // [1, 2, &quot;Fizz&quot;]</pre>\n<h2>The task: FizzBuzz one step at a time</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty output array and a fizzBuzz function that only logs output.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Every call appends the next FizzBuzz item to output and prints the array.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create count = 1 beside the output array.</li><li>In fizzBuzz push count, increment it with count++, then log output.</li><li>Add if (count % 3 === 0) to push &quot;Fizz&quot; instead of the number.</li><li>Add else if (count % 5 === 0) to push &quot;Buzz&quot;.</li><li>Notice 15 gives Fizz only; move the combined (&amp;&amp;) check to the top to produce &quot;FizzBuzz&quot;.</li><li>Call the function repeatedly (15+ times) and verify the output.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> After 15 calls: [1, 2, &quot;Fizz&quot;, 4, &quot;Buzz&quot;, &quot;Fizz&quot;, 7, 8, &quot;Fizz&quot;, &quot;Buzz&quot;, 11, &quot;Fizz&quot;, 13, 14, &quot;FizzBuzz&quot;].</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var output = [];\nvar count = 1;\n\nfunction fizzBuzz() {\n  if (count % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; count % 5 === 0) {\n    output.push(&quot;FizzBuzz&quot;);\n  } else if (count % 3 === 0) {\n    output.push(&quot;Fizz&quot;);\n  } else if (count % 5 === 0) {\n    output.push(&quot;Buzz&quot;);\n  } else {\n    output.push(count);\n  }\n  count++;\n  console.log(output);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting count++, so the same number is pushed forever.</li><li>Putting the 3 check before the combined 3-and-5 check.</li><li>Declaring output or count inside the function so they reset.</li><li>Writing = instead of === in the modulo test.</li><li>The reference repo's version derives the number from output.length and pops and re-pushes; it works but differs from the counter-based approach in the lecture.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l107", "<p class=\"summary\">Solution walkthrough: pick a random element from an array of any length by using names.length to scale Math.random(), flooring it to get a valid index, and returning a sentence.</p>\n<h3>Picking a random array item</h3>\n<ul><li>Array positions run from 0 to length - 1; names[0] is the first and names[4] the last in a 5-item array.</li><li>The function cannot assume a fixed size, so read the size with names.length and store it, e.g. numberOfPeople.</li><li>Math.random() * numberOfPeople yields 0 up to just under the length (for 5 people, 0 to 4.999...).</li><li>Math.floor() of that gives an integer from 0 to length - 1, exactly the valid indices.</li><li>Use that index in square brackets to fetch the name.</li></ul>\n<h3>Returning the result</h3>\n<ul><li>Return (not console.log) the name concatenated with the rest of the sentence: &quot; is going to buy lunch today!&quot;.</li><li>The leading space in the string literal separates the name from the text.</li><li>Test by calling the function repeatedly, with the same or different arrays.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Step-by-step solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function whosPaying(names) {\n  var numberOfPeople = names.length;\n  var randomPersonPosition = Math.floor(Math.random() * numberOfPeople);\n  var randomPerson = names[randomPersonPosition];\n  return randomPerson + &quot; is going to buy lunch today!&quot;;\n}\n\nconsole.log(whosPaying([&quot;Angela&quot;, &quot;Ben&quot;, &quot;Jenny&quot;, &quot;Michael&quot;, &quot;Chloe&quot;]));</pre>\n<h2>The task: Who's buying lunch</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A whosPaying(names) function stub and an array of names.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Return &quot;&lt;random name&gt; is going to buy lunch today!&quot;.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Read the array size with names.length.</li><li>Compute Math.floor(Math.random() * length) for a random index.</li><li>Look up names[index].</li><li>Return the name plus &quot; is going to buy lunch today!&quot; with the leading space.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each call returns a sentence naming a randomly chosen person from the list.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var nameList = [&quot;Angela&quot;, &quot;Ben&quot;, &quot;Jenny&quot;, &quot;Michael&quot;, &quot;Chloe&quot;];\n\nfunction whosPaying(names) {\n  var drawnNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * names.length);\n  return names[drawnNumber] + &quot; is going to buy lunch today!&quot;;\n}\n\nconsole.log(whosPaying(nameList));</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Hard-coding 5 instead of names.length.</li><li>Using length as the maximum index (off by one).</li><li>Forgetting the space before the sentence text.</li><li>Logging instead of returning.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-bottles", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the while loop, which repeats a block while its condition is true. Used to make FizzBuzz fill the array up to 100 in one call, shows how an infinite loop freezes the tab, and sets the 99 Bottles of Beer challenge.</p>\n<h3>How a while loop runs</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: while (condition) { body }. The condition is checked before every pass; the body runs again and again until the condition is false.</li><li>Step through: i = 1; check i &lt; 2 (true); run body (log i, then i++ so i is 2); jump back; check again, now false, so skip to the code after the loop.</li><li>The body must change something that the condition depends on, otherwise it never ends.</li><li>If the condition is false from the start, the body never runs at all.</li></ul>\n<h3>Applying it to FizzBuzz</h3>\n<ul><li>Wrap the existing if / else if chain in while (count &lt;= 100) { ... }.</li><li>Keep count++ inside the loop after the checks, so each pass handles one number and moves on.</li><li>One call to fizzBuzz now fills output with 100 items. When count reaches 101 the condition fails and execution continues to console.log(output) after the loop.</li><li>The editor highlights the matching brace to show exactly what is inside the loop body.</li></ul>\n<h3>Infinite loops</h3>\n<ul><li>While loops are error-prone: forgetting count++ leaves count at 1, so count &lt;= 100 stays true forever.</li><li>In the browser this hangs or crashes the tab and disconnects DevTools. Reloading the page recovers.</li><li>Try it deliberately once in a throwaway snippet so you recognise the symptom later.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Minimal while loop</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var i = 1;\nwhile (i &lt; 2) {\n  console.log(i);   // 1\n  i++;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">FizzBuzz to 100</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var output = [];\nvar count = 1;\n\nfunction fizzBuzz() {\n  while (count &lt;= 100) {\n    if (count % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; count % 5 === 0) {\n      output.push(&quot;FizzBuzz&quot;);\n    } else if (count % 3 === 0) {\n      output.push(&quot;Fizz&quot;);\n    } else if (count % 5 === 0) {\n      output.push(&quot;Buzz&quot;);\n    } else {\n      output.push(count);\n    }\n    count++;\n  }\n  console.log(output);\n}\nfizzBuzz();</pre>\n<h2>The task: 99 Bottles of Beer (introduced)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A browser DevTools snippet, with a function of your own name (for example beer).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Using a while loop, print the full lyrics of the song to the console when the function is called, decreasing the count by one each verse.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a function you can call from the console.</li><li>Use a while loop with a counter that starts at 99 and decreases.</li><li>Log each verse inside the loop instead of writing separate console.log lines.</li><li>Handle the final verse: no more bottles, go to the store, back to 99.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The console prints the whole song from 99 down to no more bottles when the function is called.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to update the loop variable, causing an infinite loop that freezes the tab.</li><li>Off-by-one conditions: &lt; 100 versus &lt;= 100 changes whether 100 is processed.</li><li>Declaring count inside the loop so it resets every pass.</li><li>Reference repo version matches the lecture's final FizzBuzz loop.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l109", "<p class=\"summary\">A short written solution to the 99 Bottles of Beer challenge using a while loop that counts down from 99. It ends with an optional stretch goal about correct singular/plural grammar and the 'no more' final line.</p>\n<h3>Shape of the while-loop solution</h3>\n<ul><li>Store the starting count in a variable (99) and run a while loop for as long as the count is still &gt;= 0 (the posted version includes 0, so a zero-bottle verse is printed too).</li><li>Each pass logs several console.log lines built by string concatenation: the count, a space, a word for 'bottle', then the rest of the verse text.</li><li>The count is reduced with the decrement operator (numberOfBottles--) part-way through the pass, so the last line of each verse shows the already-lowered number. Forgetting the decrement creates an infinite loop.</li><li>The challenge is judged on output, not on matching one exact program: any approach that prints the full song counts as a success. Sharing alternatives in the course Q&amp;A is encouraged.</li></ul>\n<h3>Singular/plural word</h3>\n<ul><li>A variable (bottleWord) holds the noun, and an if statement changes it when the count is exactly 1. Strict equality (===) is used for the check.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The posted code has the two words swapped: it starts with 'bottle' and switches to 'bottles' at 1, so the output is 'bottle' for most verses and 'bottles' at 1. The intended logic is the opposite: default to 'bottles', switch to 'bottle' when the count is 1.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The decrement happens after the first three lines but the same bottleWord is reused on the last line, so the final line's noun can be wrong after the decrement. Recompute the word after decrementing, or use a helper function.</li></ul>\n<h3>Stretch goal</h3>\n<ul><li>Make the grammar correct: print '1 bottle of beer on the wall' at 1.</li><li>At a count of 0, swap the numeral for the words 'No more' in the verse text.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Posted solution (reconstructed; note the swapped words bug)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numberOfBottles = 99;\nwhile (numberOfBottles &gt;= 0) {\n  var bottleWord = &quot;bottle&quot;;\n  if (numberOfBottles === 1) {\n    bottleWord = &quot;bottles&quot;;   // bug: should be the other way round\n  }\n  console.log(numberOfBottles + &quot; &quot; + bottleWord + &quot; of beer on the wall&quot;);\n  console.log(numberOfBottles + &quot; &quot; + bottleWord + &quot; of beer,&quot;);\n  console.log(&quot;Take one down, pass it around,&quot;);\n  numberOfBottles--;\n  console.log(numberOfBottles + &quot; &quot; + bottleWord + &quot; of beer on the wall.&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Corrected version with the stretch goal</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function bottleText(n) {\n  if (n === 0) return &quot;No more bottles&quot;;\n  if (n === 1) return &quot;1 bottle&quot;;\n  return n + &quot; bottles&quot;;\n}\n\nlet n = 99;\nwhile (n &gt; 0) {\n  console.log(bottleText(n) + &quot; of beer on the wall,&quot;);\n  console.log(bottleText(n) + &quot; of beer.&quot;);\n  console.log(&quot;Take one down, pass it around,&quot;);\n  n--;\n  console.log(bottleText(n) + &quot; of beer on the wall.&quot;);\n  console.log(&quot;&quot;);\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: 99 Bottles of Beer (solution and stretch goal)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script.js; the earlier while-loop lecture set the challenge.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Print the lyrics of the song from 99 down, then optionally fix grammar for 1 and 0.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a counter starting at 99.</li><li>Loop with while while the counter is above the stopping value.</li><li>Log the verse lines, joining the counter and noun with +.</li><li>Decrement the counter inside the loop.</li><li>Stretch: pick 'bottle' vs 'bottles' with an if, and print 'No more' for 0.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Console shows verses from 99 downwards ending with 0/no more bottles.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function beer() {\n    var i = 98;\n    while (i &gt;= 1) {\n        var text = (i + 1) + &quot; bottles of beer on the wall, &quot; + (i + 1) + &quot; bottles of beer. Take one down and pass it around, &quot; + i + &quot; bottle of beer on the wall.&quot;;\n        i--;\n        console.log(text);\n    }\n    console.log(&quot;1 bottle of beer on the wall, 1 bottle of beer. Take one down and pass it around, no more bottles of beer on the wall.&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;No more bottles of beer on the wall, no more bottles of beer. Go to the store and buy some more, 99 bottles of beer on the wall.&quot;);\n}\nbeer();</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Infinite loop if the counter is never decremented.</li><li>Singular/plural logic inverted (as in the posted code).</li><li>Using the stale noun after the decrement.</li><li>Off-by-one: &gt;= 0 vs &gt; 0 changes whether a zero verse prints.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: it hard-codes the 99 and 'bottle' on the final line and ends the song with the 'go to the store' line; it is a student's variant, not the lecture's code.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-for", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the for loop as a compact rearrangement of the while loop, walks through exactly when each of its three parts runs, and converts the FizzBuzz function from while to for. Ends with guidance on choosing between while and for.</p>\n<h3>Anatomy of a for loop</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: the keyword for, then parentheses holding three parts separated by semicolons, then a block in curly braces.</li><li>Part 1, initialisation: declare and set the counter, e.g. var i = 0. It runs once, before the first round.</li><li>Part 2, condition: a boolean test such as i &lt; 2. It is evaluated before every round, including the first; when it is false the loop ends and execution continues on the next line after the block.</li><li>Part 3, update: how the counter changes after each round, e.g. i++, i-- or a step of 2 or 3 (i += 2). It runs at the end of the block, right at the closing brace, not at the start.</li><li>A for loop is 'syntactic sugar' for a while loop: the counter creation, the exit test and the counter change are all gathered in the header instead of being spread around the loop.</li></ul>\n<h3>Trace of a one-round loop</h3>\n<ul><li>Start: create i = 1. Check 1 &lt; 2: true, so run the body, which logs 1.</li><li>End of the body: the update runs and i becomes 2. Jump back to the test: 2 &lt; 2 is false, so the loop exits.</li><li>Takeaway: the condition is checked first each time, so a loop whose condition is false from the start never runs its body.</li></ul>\n<h3>Converting the FizzBuzz function from while to for</h3>\n<ul><li>Replace the keyword while with for.</li><li>Move the counter declaration (starting at 1) from above the loop into the first slot of the header.</li><li>Condition: keep going up to and including 100.</li><li>Update: increment by 1 each time, so the line that changed the counter inside the old while body is removed.</li><li>Calling the function gives identical output to the while version, with less code, which is why most programmers prefer for when the number of rounds is known.</li></ul>\n<h3>Direction and step size</h3>\n<ul><li>Counting down: start high, use a decrement as the update (count--) and test a lower bound, e.g. start at 100 and stop once it is no longer above 1.</li><li>Any step is allowed (by 2, 3, etc.), so for loops handle any counting pattern.</li></ul>\n<h3>while vs for</h3>\n<ul><li>while tests a state: keep repeating while something remains true (e.g. while a player is alive) when the number of rounds is not known in advance.</li><li>for iterates: run code a defined number of times, with the counter set up in the header.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Either can express the other; pick the one that reads most clearly for the job.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">For loop that logs once (trace example)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">for (var i = 1; i &lt; 2; i++) {\n  console.log(i);   // runs once with i = 1\n}\n// after the body i++ makes i 2, test 2 &lt; 2 fails, loop ends</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">FizzBuzz with a for loop (reference repo, tidied)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var output = [];\n\nfunction fizzBuzz() {\n  for (var i = 1; i &lt;= 100; i++) {\n    if (i % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; i % 5 === 0) {\n      output.push(&quot;FizzBuzz&quot;);\n    } else if (i % 3 === 0) {\n      output.push(&quot;Fizz&quot;);\n    } else if (i % 5 === 0) {\n      output.push(&quot;Buzz&quot;);\n    } else {\n      output.push(i);\n    }\n  }\n  console.log(output);\n}\nfizzBuzz();</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Counting down and stepping by 2</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">for (var count = 100; count &gt;= 1; count--) { /* 100 down to 1 */ }\nfor (var n = 0; n &lt;= 10; n += 2) { /* 0,2,4,...,10 */ }</pre>\n<h2>The task: Refactor FizzBuzz from while to for</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The FizzBuzz function from the earlier while-loop lecture, with a counter declared before the loop and changed inside it.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Rewrite it using a for loop and confirm the output is unchanged.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Change while to for.</li><li>Move the counter declaration into the first part of the header.</li><li>Put the end condition (up to 100) as the second part.</li><li>Add the increment as the third part and delete the old increment line from the body.</li><li>Call fizzBuzz() and compare the result with the while version.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Same FizzBuzz sequence for 1 to 100 as before, using fewer lines.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">for (var i = 1; i &lt;= 100; i++) {\n  if (i % 3 === 0 &amp;&amp; i % 5 === 0) output.push(&quot;FizzBuzz&quot;);\n  else if (i % 3 === 0) output.push(&quot;Fizz&quot;);\n  else if (i % 5 === 0) output.push(&quot;Buzz&quot;);\n  else output.push(i);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Believing the update runs at the start of a round; it runs after the body.</li><li>Using &lt; versus &lt;= and ending one iteration early or late.</li><li>Forgetting semicolons (not commas) between the three header parts.</li><li>Counting down but writing i++ (never ends) or a condition that is false immediately (never runs).</li><li>Reference repo diverges slightly: it declares output globally and uses var; fine, but let is preferred in modern code.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s15-fibonacci", "<p class=\"summary\">Briefs the Fibonacci coding challenge: write fibonacciGenerator(n) that returns an array of the first n Fibonacci numbers. Lays out the checker's constraints and sketches the flowchart logic as a hint.</p>\n<h3>The sequence</h3>\n<ul><li>Begin with 0 and 1; every later term is the sum of the two terms before it (1+2=3, 2+3=5, 13+21=34 style).</li><li>This course's version always starts 0, 1 (other versions start at 1), so the first two entries are fixed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Function contract</h3>\n<ul><li>Call shape: fibonacciGenerator(n), where n is how many items to produce.</li><li>The result is returned (not alerted, no prompt) as an array: fibonacciGenerator(3) gives [0, 1, 1]; fibonacciGenerator(5) gives five items [0, 1, 1, 2, 3].</li><li>It must also work for n = 1 ([0]), n = 2 ([0, 1]) and large n such as 245.</li><li>A variable (output) holds the returned array for testing: pass a number, run, inspect the array.</li></ul>\n<h3>Checker constraints in the exercise</h3>\n<ul><li>Do not edit the existing scaffold code; the checker looks for the function name and calls it with a number.</li><li>Return a real array with square brackets and comma-separated numbers.</li><li>If using a for loop, declare the counter inside the header with var (for (var i = 0; ...)), not an undeclared i = 0, because the checker's JavaScript version demands it.</li><li>A while loop or any other approach is allowed.</li><li>An online sandbox is linked from the lesson for trying the code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Planning with a flowchart</h3>\n<ul><li>The hard part is the logic, not the syntax. Draw it first (a tool such as draw.io works) and then turn it into code.</li><li>Flow: if n is 1, return [0]. If n is 2, return [0, 1]. These are the predefined starting values.</li><li>Otherwise start from the first values and add the last two items to get the next one, push it on the array.</li><li>After each addition compare the array length to n; if smaller, repeat; if equal, return the array.</li><li>Worked example n = 5: [0,1] then add 0+1 -&gt; 3 items, add 1+1 -&gt; 4 items, add 1+2 -&gt; 5 items, stop.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Expected behaviour of the target function</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">fibonacciGenerator(1); // [0]\nfibonacciGenerator(2); // [0, 1]\nfibonacciGenerator(3); // [0, 1, 1]\nfibonacciGenerator(5); // [0, 1, 1, 2, 3]</pre>\n<h2>The task: Coding Exercise 7: Fibonacci</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A scaffold with a fibonacciGenerator(n) function stub and a variable that stores its output; do not alter these.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Return an array containing the first n Fibonacci numbers.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Handle n = 1 by returning [0].</li><li>Handle n = 2 by returning [0, 1].</li><li>For larger n, begin with [0, 1].</li><li>Loop, adding the last two entries and pushing the result, until the array has n items.</li><li>Return the array.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> fibonacciGenerator(5) returns [0, 1, 1, 2, 3]; works for n = 1, 2 and 245.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function fibonacciGenerator(n) {\n    var sequenceOfNumbers = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        if (i === 0) {\n            sequenceOfNumbers.push(0);\n        } else if (i === 1) {\n            sequenceOfNumbers.push(1);\n        } else {\n            sequenceOfNumbers.push(sequenceOfNumbers[i - 1] + sequenceOfNumbers[i - 2]);\n        }\n    }\n    return sequenceOfNumbers;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Editing the scaffold, which breaks the checker.</li><li>Using alert/console.log instead of return.</li><li>Starting the sequence at 1.</li><li>Writing i = 0 without var in the for header.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> n = 0 is not covered by the lecture; return [] for it.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: it has a hard-coded 'var number = 1' and console.log call outside the function.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l112", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through turning the Fibonacci flowchart into code: base cases with if / else if, a hand-written sum of the last two items, then replacing the repetition with a for loop that uses output.length for the last two positions.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: base cases from the flowchart</h3>\n<ul><li>Create output as an empty array inside the function.</li><li>if (n === 1) set output to [0].</li><li>else if (n === 2) set output to [0, 1]. An else if is only evaluated when the earlier condition was false.</li><li>else branch handles every larger n: start output at [0, 1], then add more terms.</li><li>All branches fall through to a single return output at the end.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: first manual sum</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside else, the next number is output[0] + output[1] (0 + 1 = 1) and is appended with output.push(...); the array becomes [0, 1, 1].</li><li>The flowchart then asks whether n equals the array's length; if yes return, otherwise add again. This suggests an if (n === output.length) check.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: make the indexes dynamic</h3>\n<ul><li>Hard-coded positions 0 and 1 only work for a two-item array. Use output.length - 1 for the last item and output.length - 2 for the second last; this works at any size (length 4 gives positions 3 and 2).</li><li>length is a property, so no parentheses (the lecture's spoken output.length() is loose wording).</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: collapse the repetition into a loop</h3>\n<ul><li>Repeated lines plus the 'go back and check again' arrow are the signal to use a loop.</li><li>for loop: counter starts at 2 (two items already exist), condition i &lt; n, step i++. Each round pushes the sum of the last two items.</li><li>The loop ends when i reaches n, which leaves exactly n items. The loop condition replaces the explicit length-vs-n check.</li><li>Test with n = 1, 2 and 5 in the sandbox, then run the course checker.</li></ul>\n<h3>Closing advice</h3>\n<ul><li>Many routes work (while loop, fewer if branches); correct output meeting the stated rules is what counts, and the best solution is one the learner understands.</li><li>Common sticking points flagged: array methods and remembering to loop.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final solution (matches the lecture's approach)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function fibonacciGenerator(n) {\n  var output = [];\n  if (n === 1) {\n    output = [0];\n  } else if (n === 2) {\n    output = [0, 1];\n  } else {\n    output = [0, 1];\n    for (var i = 2; i &lt; n; i++) {\n      output.push(output[output.length - 1] + output[output.length - 2]);\n    }\n  }\n  return output;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference-repo variant: a single loop with index checks, no separate base cases</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function fibonacciGenerator(n) {\n  var sequenceOfNumbers = [];\n  for (var i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n    if (i === 0) {\n      sequenceOfNumbers.push(0);\n    } else if (i === 1) {\n      sequenceOfNumbers.push(1);\n    } else {\n      sequenceOfNumbers.push(sequenceOfNumbers[i - 1] + sequenceOfNumbers[i - 2]);\n    }\n  }\n  return sequenceOfNumbers;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Fibonacci solution walkthrough</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> fibonacciGenerator(n) stub from the previous lecture.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Return the first n numbers of the sequence as an array.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare an empty output array.</li><li>if n is 1 return [0] via output; else if n is 2 use [0, 1].</li><li>Else start with [0, 1].</li><li>Loop i from 2 while i &lt; n.</li><li>In each round push output[output.length-1] + output[output.length-2].</li><li>Return output.</li><li>Run with n = 1, 2, 5, then use the checker.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> n=1 gives [0]; n=2 gives [0,1]; n=5 gives [0,1,1,2,3].</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function fibonacciGenerator(n) {\n  var output = [];\n  if (n === 1) {\n    output = [0];\n  } else if (n === 2) {\n    output = [0, 1];\n  } else {\n    output = [0, 1];\n    for (var i = 2; i &lt; n; i++) {\n      output.push(output[output.length - 1] + output[output.length - 2]);\n    }\n  }\n  return output;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing output.length() with parentheses (it is a property).</li><li>Starting the loop at 0 or 1 so the first terms get duplicated.</li><li>Using &lt;= n and getting n+1 items.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> n = 0 or negative: the lecture's code returns [0, 1] for n = 0; add an explicit guard if needed.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: it uses a different but valid single-loop structure.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l113", "<p class=\"summary\">A study tip: following tutorials feels easy, but solving problems alone is a different skill. Regular retrieval practice closes that gap.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Feeling that everything makes sense while watching, then blanking on a challenge, is common and expected around this stage of the course.</li><li>Watching recognises information; coding requires recalling it from memory. They are separate skills, so passive viewing alone does not build the second.</li><li>Practise retrieval often: find extra exercises online, write your own quizzes, and try variations of what a lesson showed.</li><li>Predict the next step: pause before the instructor demonstrates something and attempt it yourself first.</li><li>More active practice leads to a firmer grasp than extra listening.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Binge-watching lectures without attempting the exercise first.</li></ul>");
